# Guitar MP3 Transcription\n\nA local web app where the end-user can:\n\n1. open the website,\n2. upload an MP3,\n3. wait for processing,\n4. download a PDF guitar score/tab.\n\n## End-user goal\n\nThe user should only need to interact with the website.\nThey should not have to manually call APIs or use separate frontend/backend URLs.\n\nThis project now serves the website directly from the FastAPI app, so the tester only needs to open:\n\n- `http://127.0.0.1:8000`\n\n## What the pipeline does\n\n- Accepts MP3 upload in the browser\n- Uses Spotify `Basic Pitch` for polyphonic note estimation\n- Groups near-simultaneous notes into chord events\n- Infers guitar-friendly string/fret placements from the selected tuning\n- Exports:\n  - PDF summary score/tab\n  - JSON artifact\n  - MusicXML artifact for future higher-quality notation workflows\n\n## Accuracy notes\n\nThis is more accurate than the initial prototype because it now:\n\n- quantizes onset times before grouping,\n- merges very close note events into chord clusters,\n- exports MusicXML as an intermediate notation format,\n- keeps the website interaction simple while improving the internal pipeline.\n\nStill, it remains a heuristic transcription system. It is best for:\n\n- relatively isolated guitar recordings,\n- clear chord strums and picked passages,\n- MP3s without heavy overlap from drums, vocals, or other instruments.\n\n## Project structure\n\n- `frontend/index.html` — website UI served by backend\n- `backend/app/main.py` — FastAPI server and website host\n- `backend/app/transcription.py` — transcription and event grouping\n- `backend/app/guitar.py` — tuning and fretboard inference\n- `backend/app/musicxml_export.py` — MusicXML generation\n- `backend/app/pdf_render.py` — PDF output generation\n- `run.sh` — one-command local startup\n- `package_for_friend.sh` — creates a zip for sharing\n\n## Local startup\n\nFrom the project root:\n\n```bash\nchmod +x run.sh\n./run.sh\n```\n\nThen open:\n\n- `http://127.0.0.1:8000`\n\n## What your friend needs installed\n\nBecause this is not Dockerized, your friend should have:\n\n- Python 3.10+ recommended\n- `pip`\n- system libraries required by `basic-pitch` and its dependencies if prompted during install\n\nIn many cases, this is enough:\n\n```bash\ncd guitar-mp3-transcription\nchmod +x run.sh\n./run.sh\n```\n\nThe script will:\n\n- create a virtual environment if needed,\n- install Python dependencies,\n- start the app.\n\n## How to package and send to a friend\n\n### Option 1: create the zip with the included script\n\nFrom the project root:\n\n```bash\nchmod +x package_for_friend.sh\n./package_for_friend.sh\n```\n\nThat creates:\n\n- `guitar-mp3-transcription-test-package.zip`\n\nYou can send that zip to your friend.\n\n### Option 2: manually compress the folder\n\nFrom the parent directory:\n\n```bash\nzip -r guitar-mp3-transcription.zip guitar-mp3-transcription -x \"*/.venv/*\" \"*/__pycache__/*\" \"*/outputs/*\" \"*/uploads/*\"\n```\n\n## Instructions for your friend\n\n1. Unzip the project\n2. Open a terminal in the unzipped folder\n3. Run:\n\n```bash\nchmod +x run.sh\n./run.sh\n```\n\n4. Open a browser to:\n\n- `http://127.0.0.1:8000`\n\n5. Upload an MP3 and click **Generate PDF**\n6. Download the resulting PDF from the website\n\n